⭐ GraphQL Simply Explained
✔️ GraphQL is ONE API endpoint

Instead of many URLs (like /users, /orders, /products), GraphQL uses just one endpoint, usually:

/graphql

✔️ YOU tell it exactly what information you want

You write a query to specify:

which object you want

which fields you want

how deep you want to go

✔️ The server gives back ONLY what you asked for

Not extra fields.
Not hidden information (unless it’s insecure).
Just exactly what you request.

🍔 REST vs GraphQL — SUPER SIMPLE
🍟 REST (normal APIs)

You have MANY URLs:

/api/user/5
/api/user/5/orders
/api/products


Each URL gives a different kind of data.

🍣 GraphQL

You have ONE URL:

/graphql


And YOU write a question:

{
  user(id: 5) {
    name
    email
  }
}


This pulls ONLY name and email — nothing else.

🎯 Think of it like ordering food

REST:

You can only order whatever is on the menu exactly as written.

GraphQL:

You customize your order:
“I want a burger but only cheese and pickles.”

YOU decide the shape of the data.

🧠 Another easy way to think of it

GraphQL is like:

One big folder (endpoint)

Inside it is ALL the app's data

You ask for EXACTLY the pieces you want

🕵🏽‍♀️ Why it matters for bug bounty

Because if YOU can ask for data…

Sometimes you can ask for:

password

email

tokens

other users’ info

And the server accidentally gives it to you.

Boom — IDOR/BOLA.

🧸 The Easiest Definition

Here is the simplest one:

GraphQL is one API that lets you choose exactly what data you want from it.

That’s it.
